<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mpondo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amaMpondo, Pondo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mpondo are a Xhosa-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> people of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, in the region historically called Pondoland, between the Mthatha and Mtamvuna rivers. Their kingdom kept its independence until it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1894. In 1960 the Mpondo revolt against apartheid's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a> policy was crushed by the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpondo.jpg" alt="Mpondo" width="317" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Geddes Bain (1797 - 1864) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bain_-_Mpondo_haarmodes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
